# 2. DESIGN EXPERIENCE

Imagine a clinician or researcher wearing a spatial headset.

A pair of transparent lungs appears approximately at arm's length.

The pulmonary vascular system is visible within the lungs.

A region of obstruction is highlighted in engineering gold.

The user reaches toward that region, selects it, and enlarges the affected artery.

The system smoothly transitions from an anatomical view to a cutaway vessel.

Within the vessel, the user sees a modeled fibrin-rich thrombus containing red blood cells and platelets.

Another action isolates only the fibrin network.

The network enlarges again, revealing individual strands, branches, junctions, and surrounding clot components.

The researcher can then activate a time-dependent visualization of simulated clot remodeling, measure modeled structural changes, and return to the whole-lung view.

Every transition must preserve anatomical location, visual continuity, and scientific context.

## Design principle

The product must support genuine spatial investigation.

Do not build a static image placed inside a 3D room.

All primary anatomical structures must be actual navigable 3D geometry with independent materials, visibility controls, selection, transforms, and metadata.

