The viewer must offer both external and internal inspection.

An internal camera mode should allow the user to look along the vessel without implying that a real optical camera generated these visuals.

## Scale C — Clot Architecture

Represent a fibrin-rich thrombus as a three-dimensional composition of interconnected components.

Components include:

- Fibrin fiber network.
- Red blood cells.
- Platelets.
- Optional leukocyte populations.
- A conceptual distinction between more densely packed and less densely packed clot regions.
- Local void spaces.
- Fiber intersections.
- Fiber bundles.
- Model-defined structural connections.

Each component is independently selectable.

Preserve distinct visual appearances and scientifically labeled material identities.

## Scale D — Fibrin Microstructure

Create a true 3D network of thin fibers.

The network should allow users to investigate:

- Fiber paths.
- Fiber diameter distributions.
- Branching and intersections.
- Fiber orientation.
- Network connectivity.
- Mesh pore geometry.
- Spatial heterogeneity.
- Local fiber concentration.
- Synthetic deformation under applied loads.

Distinguish physical cross-linking from apparent geometric intersection.

Do not assume every point where two visual strands cross is a genuine molecular bond.

Scale D is microscopic and generally cannot be derived from patient CT alone.

