# 9. FIBRINOLYSIS AND CLOT REMODELING

Create a research visualization exploring clot breakdown.

Represent the biological relationship between:

- Plasminogen.
- Plasmin.
- Fibrin.
- Plasminogen activators.
- Fibrin degradation.

The simulation should allow conceptual observation of fibers losing connectivity and the modeled network changing shape.

Provide three distinct modes.

### Mode A — Natural Fibrinolysis

Show an educational model of endogenous clot remodeling.

### Mode B — Thrombolytic Mechanism

Illustrate how plasminogen activation can contribute to fibrin degradation.

Clearly indicate that thrombolytic medications carry serious bleeding risks and are only appropriate in selected clinical circumstances.

### Mode C — Mechanical Clot Removal

Show a separate, conceptual visualization of an established catheter-based thrombectomy mechanism.

Do not portray noninvasive external sensing as a proven means of dissolving or removing pulmonary clots.

The research viewer may compare mechanical removal and pharmacological fibrinolysis conceptually, but it must not generate personalized treatment recommendations.

## Fiber removal algorithm

For animation:

- Store mesh connectivity in the fibrin network graph.
- Select subsets of edges according to a named illustrative degradation model.
- Update node-edge connectivity.
- Recalculate connected components.
- Change the rendered mesh.
- Illustrate disconnected fragments where relevant.

Avoid implying that visual deletion of fibers precisely represents the rate or spatial pattern of actual fibrinolysis.

Allow experimental degradation data to be imported later.

